title: Fullscreen Editing
modified: 20261114000000000
created: 20261114000000000

A toggle that makes the tiddler you are editing fill the window, like a modal. Off by default; turn it on per-session with the ⛶ button in the edit-mode toolbar, next to save/cancel/delete.

!! The moving parts

Two tiddlers of mine, plus one state tiddler that appears on first use.

;[[$:/my/fullscreen-editor-css]]
: Tagged `$:/tags/Stylesheet`. All the CSS. Every rule is wrapped in a `<$list>` gate so it only applies when the state tiddler says `yes`.
;[[$:/my/Buttons/fullscreen-editing]]
: Tagged `$:/tags/EditToolbar`, which is the toolbar in a tiddler's title bar while editing (''not'' `$:/tags/EditorToolbar`, which is the bold/italic row above the text area). Flips the state tiddler and swaps its own icon.
;`$:/state/my/fullscreen-editor`
: Holds the ''title of the draft'' that is currently fullscreened, e.g. `Draft of 'Foo'`, or `none` when off. Created on first click. Lives under `$:/state/` so it is ''saved with the wiki'' — the setting is sticky across reloads. Move it to `$:/temp/` if you would rather it reset each session.

!! How the scoping works

The CSS targets ''one specific draft'' rather than every edit frame, which is what stops the wrong tiddler going fullscreen when two are open.

The edit frame carries the draft title in an attribute — `$:/core/ui/EditTemplate` renders `data-tiddler-title=<<currentTiddler>>`, and in edit mode `currentTiddler` is the draft. So the stylesheet builds a selector from the state tiddler:

```
<$list filter="[[$:/state/my/fullscreen-editor]get[text]!match[none]]" variable="ignore">
<$let fs={{{ [[$:/state/my/fullscreen-editor]get[text]escapecss[]] }}}>
.tc-tiddler-edit-frame[data-tiddler-title=<<fs>>] { ... }
</$let>
</$list>
```

`escapecss` is what makes an arbitrary title safe in a selector — `Draft of 'Foo'` comes out as `Draft\ of\ \'Foo\'`. Core uses the same trick in `$:/core/ui/EditorToolbar/link-dropdown`. The attribute value is deliberately ''unquoted'', because that is what the escaping is designed for.

The stylesheet needs this pragma, without which a widget inside a stylesheet renders as literal text:

```
\rules only filteredtranscludeinline transcludeinline macrodef macrocallinline macrocallblock html commentinline commentblock
```

`html` parses the widgets, `filteredtranscludeinline` parses `{{{ ... }}}`, and `macrocallinline` is what lets `<<fs>>` and `<<colour tiddler-background>>` resolve. Palette macros work here because `$:/core/ui/PageStylesheet` wraps every stylesheet in `\import [subfilter{$:/core/config/GlobalImportFilter}]`.

The button toggles by storing its own draft title, using `none` as the off value:

```
<$action-setfield $tiddler="$:/state/my/fullscreen-editor"
  $value={{{ [[$:/state/my/fullscreen-editor]get[text]match<currentTiddler>then[none]else<currentTiddler>] }}}/>
```

Three cases, all of which fall out of that one filter: already fullscreen in this draft → `none`; fullscreen in a different draft → switches to this one; state missing entirely → the `else` operator fires on the empty run and supplies this draft's title.

!! Turning it off

* Click the button again — it is a toggle, and the icon becomes the fold icon while fullscreen is active.
* Save (cmd-Enter) or cancel (Escape) the draft. Edit mode ends, `.tc-tiddler-edit-frame` stops existing, the CSS stops matching. The state stays `yes`, so the ''next'' edit is fullscreen again.
* Emergency, from the browser console: `$tw.wiki.setText("$:/state/my/fullscreen-editor","text",null,"no")`

!! Gotchas worth remembering

* ''Two drafts at once.'' This is why the selector is scoped to one draft title. An earlier version targeted `.tc-tiddler-edit-frame` flat, so both frames became `position: fixed` overlays at the same `z-index` and the later one in DOM order won — meaning the wrong tiddler appeared to go fullscreen.
* ''Stale state.'' After you save, the draft tiddler disappears but its title stays in the state tiddler. Harmless, since no frame matches it any more. Re-editing the same tiddler usually reproduces the same draft title, so it will come up fullscreen again.
* ''CodeMirror 6 has no textarea.'' A rule targeting `textarea.tc-edit-texteditor` is dead code under [[$:/plugins/samsmith/codemirror6]]; the editor is `.cm6tw-wrapper .cm-editor`. The `min-height` has to go on `.cm-editor`, not on the wrapper — the plugin's own stylesheet sets `.cm-editor { height: 100% }`, and a percentage height against an auto-height parent resolves to auto.
* ''Hardcoded background.'' If the overlay uses a literal hex like `#f4f3ee`, toolbar icons inherit their colour from the palette and can end up invisible on a dark palette. Prefer `<<colour tiddler-background>>`.
* ''Scrolling.'' The overlay is `overflow-y: auto`, so the title bar — and therefore the button — can scroll out of view. The sticky rule on `.tc-tiddler-edit-title` pins it.
* ''Editor-toolbar buttons can be hidden by config.'' `$:/config/EditorToolbarButtons/Visibility/<button title>` set to `hide` silently removes a button from the bold/italic row. Control Panel → Toolbars → Editor Toolbar writes it. This bit me once. The edit-mode title-bar equivalent is `$:/config/EditToolbarButtons/Visibility/<button title>`.
* ''Unlisted toolbar buttons sort last.'' `$:/tags/EditorToolbar` and `$:/tags/EditToolbar` both carry an explicit `list` field, so a new button is appended to the end unless it has a `list-before` or `list-after` field. Mine uses `list-before: $:/core/ui/Buttons/save`.

!! Adding a keyboard shortcut

The `shortcuts` field only works for buttons in the editor toolbar — the edit widget scans that toolbar's DOM for `data-tw-keyboard-shortcut` attributes. For a title-bar button, make a separate tiddler tagged `$:/tags/KeyboardShortcut` with `key: ((my-fullscreen))` and the `$action-setfield` above as its text, then define `$:/config/shortcuts/my-fullscreen` as e.g. `ctrl-shift-F`.
